\mathcal{V}_{\text{evo}}^{(t)}\in\mathfrak{V}
records the current values; it is not the operator’s domain by itself. Evolvability markers restrict edit targets through the manager, not operating-system permissions. Frozen implementations may still execute, and ordinary episode-state changes are distinct from implementation updates.

For example, adding an event to Memory changes stored episode data, whereas revising its retention policy changes a managed resource configuration. Likewise, advancing an Environment changes its current state, whereas revising transition rules changes its implementation. The former operations do not automatically create SEPL versions. Restoring a policy or rule implementation also does not rewind episode data or external effects unless those states were separately captured and restored. Creating a new resource requires a permitted registration operation; an editable target flag is not blanket authorization to execute generated code, access files, or transmit data.
